BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dhrupad Vocal Recital by Pt. Uday Bhawalkar
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T200000
UID:dhrupad-vocal-recital-by-pt-uday-bhawalkar-2026-05-30t20-00-00-04-00@a
 gakhanmuseum.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.agakhanmuseum.org/uploads/2026/05
 /pratap_uday-aspect-ratio-1600-1000-1.png?resize=1584%2C990&gravity
DESCRIPTION:Celebrating the rich traditions of Dhrupad\, this performance 
 brings together two acclaimed artists in a musical exploration of voice\, 
 rhythm\, and sound. Renowned vocalist Uday Bhawalkar is joined by master P
 akhawaj artist Pratap Awad in a performance that reflects the depth\, disc
 ipline\, and spiritual essence of Hindustani classical music.\n\nThrough e
 xpansive ragas\, intricate rhythmic dialogue\, and spontaneous musical exc
 hange\, the duo creates an experience that invites listeners into a space 
 where melody and rhythm unfold in a meditative and beautiful style.
LOCATION:Ismaili Centre Toronto\, Aga Khan Museum\, 77 Wynford Drive\, Tor
 onto\, ON M3C 1K1
URL:https://agakhanmuseum.org/whats-on/dhrupad-vocal-recital/
X-SOURCE:Aga Khan Museum
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Playing With the World: How Games Have Shaped Modern Science and T
 echnology
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T193000
UID:playing-with-the-world-how-games-have-shaped-modern-science-and-techno
 logy-2026-06-04t18-30-00-04-00@agakhanmuseum.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.agakhanmuseum.org/uploads/2026/05
 /Reunion-aspect-ratio-1600-1000.jpg?resize=1584%2C990&gravity
DESCRIPTION:From Neolithic times to the present\, humans around the world 
 have played games. Join Dr. Samuel Pizelo\, Assistant Professor at the Uni
 versity of Toronto\, as he discusses the immense and ongoing influence the
 se games have had on the development of science and technology — from ge
 ometry and statistics to computing\, artificial intelligence\, and the com
 plexity sciences.\n\nBy focusing on the role of traditional games like che
 ss and backgammon in these developments\, we can understand science and te
 chnology not as a series of individual discoveries\, but as a shared globa
 l heritage.
LOCATION:Aga Khan Museum\, 77 Wynford Drive\, Toronto\, ON M3C 1K1
URL:https://agakhanmuseum.org/whats-on/playing-with-the-world/
X-SOURCE:Aga Khan Museum
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Red Revue Concert
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260620T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260620T193000
UID:red-revue-concert-2026-06-20t19-30-00-04-00@agakhanmuseum.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.agakhanmuseum.org/uploads/2026/05
 /Buffalo-Charging-Drum-Summer-Solstice-Alnoor-Meralli-02-scaled-aspect-rat
 io-1600-1000-scaled.jpg?resize=1584%2C990&gravity
DESCRIPTION:Join the Aga Khan Museum and the Toronto Council Fire Native C
 ultural Centre in celebrating the Summer Solstice ahead of National Indige
 nous Peoples’ Day. The Red Revue Concert\, co-presented with the Associa
 tion for Native Development in the Performing and Visual Arts (ANDPVA)\, b
 rings together traditional and contemporary Indigenous artists to showcase
  their exceptional gifts and talents.\n\nStay after the concert for a spec
 ial afterparty from 10 pm–12 am. Enjoy music in the Atrium and delicious
  snacks from the Courtyard Café\, as well as access to the Museum’s gal
 leries.
LOCATION:Nanji Family Foundation Auditorium\, Aga Khan Museum\, 77 Wynford
  Drive\, Toronto\, ON M3C 1K1
URL:https://agakhanmuseum.org/whats-on/red-revue-concert/
X-SOURCE:Aga Khan Museum
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rhythms of Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260701T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260701T173000
UID:rhythms-of-canada-2026-07-01t10-00-00-04-00@agakhanmuseum.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.agakhanmuseum.org/uploads/2026/04
 /Rhythms-of-Canada-2024-Day-2-Alnoor-Meralli-013-scaled-aspect-ratio-1600-
 1000-scaled.jpg?resize=1584%2C990&gravity
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate the diverse tapestry we call home at the Rhythms of 
 Canada f estival\, a vibrant\, free\, family-friendly cultural celebration
  returning on Wednesday\, July 1\, 2026\, across the Aga Khan Museum\, Aga
  Khan Park\, and the Ismaili Centre Toronto.\n\nTaking place on Canada Day
 \, the f estival invites visitors to experience a celebration of cultures\
 , creativity\, and community\, transforming the site into a true w orld in
  a c ity.\n\nThroughout the day\, the festival comes alive with a dynamic 
 program featuring main stage concerts\, live local performances\, and comm
 unity-led activations. In the spirit of sport\, dancers and artists rooted
  in diverse cultural traditions perform and engage with audiences across t
 he site\, celebrating movement\, heritage\, and cultural expression. Visit
 ors can also explore hands-on arts and crafts\, participate in interactive
  workshops\, and enjoy carnival and game-themed experiences designed to sp
 ark curiosity\, play\, and connection. Festival-goers will also enjoy free
  access to the Museum Collections Gallery.
LOCATION:Aga Khan Museum\, 77 Wynford Drive\, Toronto\, ON M3C 1K1
URL:https://agakhanmuseum.org/whats-on/rhythms-of-canada/
X-SOURCE:Aga Khan Museum
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:2026 Summer Camps
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
UID:2026-summer-camps-2026-07-06@agakhanmuseum.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://images.agakhanmuseum.org/uploads/2026/03
 /Camp-March-2025-Madalina-Raduta6-scaled-aspect-ratio-1600-1000-scaled.jpe
 g?resize=1584%2C990&gravity
DESCRIPTION:Inspired by the Aga Khan Museum’s collections and current ex
 hibition Game On ! \, campers ages 6 to 12 will explore the art\, strategy
 \, and creativity behind games from around the world. Throughout the summe
 r\, campers will design and build their own games\, craft handmade boards 
 and pieces\, experiment with puzzles and storytelling\, and participate in
  collaborative challenges that combine creativity\, problem-solving\, and 
 imagination.\n\nEach week introduces a new theme — from global games and
  strategy challenges to imaginative storytelling and game design — givin
 g campers the opportunity to create custom board games\, sculpt game piece
 s\, build puzzles\, invent characters\, and test their ideas through playf
 ul competition. Through hands-on making\, gallery exploration\, and outdoo
 r activities\, campers will discover how games connect cultures\, inspire 
 creativity\, and bring people together.
LOCATION:Aga Khan Museum\, 77 Wynford Drive\, Toronto\, ON M3C 1K1
URL:https://agakhanmuseum.org/whats-on/2026-summer-camps-2/
X-SOURCE:Aga Khan Museum
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
